== Guarding Each Other’s Health, Reputation, and Property

=== §. 29. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual care over one another’s body.]

After the good of the soul follows the good of the body, in which husband and wife must show their provident care for each other, and do what lies in them to further the welfare of one another’s person, and to nourish and cherish one another’s body. The apostle lays down this duty under the comparison of a body, which he calls #emph[flesh,] saying, “No man ever yet hated his own flesh, but nourishes and cherishes it.”#footnote[Eph 5:29.] Now man and wife are “one flesh.”#footnote[Eph 5:31.] The apostle applies this duty in particular to husbands. At the first institution of marriage it was applied in particular to the wife, whom God made to be “a helper suitable for” man.#footnote[Gen 2:18.] So it is a mutual duty belonging to both. It seems that Rebekah#footnote[Gen 27:14.] was so attentive to Isaac in this respect that she could readily make “tasty food” for him, such as he loved.

This duty extends to every condition, both of prosperity and adversity, of health and sickness. For they mutually covenant and promise as much when they are first joined together in marriage: “I take you,” each says to the other, “for better, for worse, for richer, for poorer, in sickness and in health, to love and to cherish.” Therefore they ought mutually both to rejoice in one another’s welfare, and also in all distress to help and comfort each other, putting their shoulders under one another’s burden, and helping to ease one another as much as they can. What Solomon said of a friend and a brother may fitly be applied in this case to husband and wife: “A friend loves at all times, and a brother is born for adversity.” #footnote[Prov 17:17.] That is, a trusty and faithful friend is constant in his goodwill, and ready to perform all duties of kindness at any time, whether in prosperity or adversity. The change of outward condition makes no alteration in his loving affection and friendly conduct. Yes, he seems to be as it were born and brought forth for the time of trouble and affliction, because then his provident care and tender affection are most clearly shown. Of all friends, none ought to be more caring, none more faithful to one another, than man and wife. How much, then, ought they to love at all times, and, if any trial comes to either of them, the other to conduct himself so that it may truly be said they were joined together for adversity!

In this way they will confirm the truth of that which moved God to create mankind male and female, namely that “it is not good for the man to be alone,”#footnote[Gen 2:18.] but that it was rather good, that is, needful, profitable and strengthening, for man and woman to be together. In this respect the wise man says, “He who finds a wife finds a good thing”;#footnote[Prov 18:22.] and by the rule of relation we may infer, #emph[she who finds a husband finds a good thing.]

=== §. 30. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ reluctance to help one another in time of need.]

Contrary to this duty is a certain lack of natural affection in various husbands and wives, who greatly begrudge providing the things that are needful for one another. The man commonly thinks the cost too great, the woman thinks the pains too much. They are disposed toward one another as if they were mere strangers; indeed, many strangers will be more ready to perform, and more cheerful in performing, needful duties (as occasion is offered) than such heartless husbands and wives. If a little sickness or some other such trial falls on one of them, the other thinks, “No one ever had such a burden,” and by their discontent they make the burden much heavier than it would otherwise be: just as when two oxen are in one yoke and one holds back, the pulling is made much harder for the other. In this way they pervert one of the principal ends of marriage, which is to be a continual comfort and #footnote[Gen 2:18.] #emph[help] to each other, and to ease the burdens of one another; in which respect they are made yokefellows. Job’s wife#footnote[Job 2:9.] by her heartless conduct toward him in his affliction greatly added to his misery. When he stood most in need of her help, she gave him the least. It appears from Job’s complaint of her, in these words, “My breath is alien to #footnote[Job 19:17.] my wife,” that she altogether neglected him in his misery. The common saying of many, after a husband or wife who has long lain sick has died, betrays their lack of natural affection. Their saying is this: “If my husband” (or wife) “had died so much sooner, I would have saved so much money.” What does this suggest but that they would have been content for their husband (or wife) to die sooner, so that they might have saved the more?

=== §. 31. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual regard for one another’s good name.]

The provident care of husbands and wives ought further to extend to the reputation and good name of one another. The good name of the wife ought to be as dear to the husband, and that of the husband to the wife, as their own. #footnote[Matt 1:19.] The great regard that Joseph had for the reputation of Mary, his betrothed wife, made him think of putting her away secretly when he observed her to be with child and did not know by whom: for he was unwilling “to make her a public example.”#footnote[παραδειγματίσαι αὐτήν.] The same regard moved Bathsheba#footnote[2 Sam 11:5.] to send secretly to David and tell him that she was with child. The commendation which the good husband#footnote[Prov 31:28. 1. A good name is precious.] (noted by Solomon) gives of his wife, being approved by the Holy Spirit, shows how man and wife ought in that respect to honor each other; and that on good grounds. For

1\. A good name is a most precious thing: “better than fine perfume,”#footnote[Eccl 7:3.] which gives a sweet scent, and #footnote[Prov 22:1.] “more desirable than great riches.” #footnote[2\. The good name of the one is the honor of the other.]

2\. So closely are husbands and wives joined together that the good name of the one cannot but tend to the honor and reputation of the other; so in this they seek their own honor also.

=== §. 32. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ preventing each other’s discredit.]

For direction in this, let us consider how this duty may be #footnote[Directions concerning a good name.] #emph[performed,] and how it may be #emph[shown.]

For the better performing of it, care must be taken both to #emph[prevent] and #emph[redress a bad name,] and also to #emph[gain] and #emph[preserve a good name.]

To #emph[prevent a bad name,] regard must be had to these three things following: #footnote[1\. Prevent a bad name, and that]

+ What one says of the other, and how.
+ What hearing they give to things reported by others.
+ What judgment they pass on one another.

For the #emph[first,] husbands and wives must in no case delight to #footnote[1\. By concealing things of ill report.] reveal to others, and spread abroad, the infirmities and imperfections of one another, or anything that may tend to the discredit of either of them; but rather cover and conceal them as much as they can with a good conscience. It is expressly said that Joseph, “being a just man,”#footnote[Matt 1:19.] labored to conceal the blemish which he imagined to be in his wife; so this may stand with #emph[justice.] Yes, it is also a part of #emph[love,] for “love covers over a multitude of sins.”#footnote[1 Pet 4:8.]

For the #emph[second,] husbands and wives must not have their #footnote[2\. By closing ears against ill reports.] ears wide open to hear every tale and report that is brought to one against the other, but rather show themselves displeased and offended with those who are ready to relate things of evil report. If a husband or wife shows a willingness to listen for tales and reports about one another, the devil will stir up instruments enough to fill their heads with tales, and those for the most part both frivolous and forged. Not only strangers, but children, servants, and those who are of the same household will always be telling some tale or other, to curry favor, as we say. But an utter dislike of such flattering talebearers will take away their occasion for telling untrue or trivial reports.

For the #emph[third,] the judgment and verdict which husbands #footnote[3\. By forbearing to censure rashly.] and wives give of one another must be either very charitable or very sparing. If one hears some notorious crime of the other reported, they must not be overhasty or quick to judge and condemn, not even though they think they see some evidence of it, but rather suspend their judgment. This seems to have been the mind of Joseph: though he observed Mary to be with #footnote[Matt 1:19.] child, yet he would not at once judge her to be a notorious adulteress, or condemn her as a hypocrite, unworthy to live, and therefore he would not make her a public example.

In brief, so that husbands and wives may be more sparing in judging one another, they must not rashly believe any evil report of one another, but rather suppress all slight suspicions as much as they can.

So that the judgment which they give of one another may be charitable, in judging they must carefully observe the properties of love, which are: 1. To interpret doubtful things in the better sense. 2. To mitigate, as far as truth and justice will allow, the faults which are evident. Michal#footnote[2 Sam 6:20.] offended against the first in a high degree, and was cursed. Abigail#footnote[1 Sam 25:25.] observed the latter, and was blessed.

=== §. 33. #emph[Of the wisdom of husbands and wives in redressing one another’s bad name.]

To #emph[redress a bad name,] husbands and wives must first give one another notice of the report that goes about concerning them, and endeavor #footnote[2\. Redress a bad name.] to work in them both a sight and also a sense of those evils which are in the mouths of others. After notice is given, they must labor to bring them to repentance for the sins for which they are badly spoken of, and to a showing of repentance by doing things fitting for repentance: that is, by a zealous and conscientious practice of such virtues as are directly contrary to the vices for which they were spoken evil of. It may with good probability be gathered from the history of the Levite,#footnote[Judg 19:1, 2, #emph[etc.]] whose wife played the whore, that he dealt with her in this way. And in this way Abigail endeavored to deal with her husband. #footnote[1 Sam 25:37.]

=== §. 34. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ care in gaining one another’s good name.]

To #emph[gain a good report,] husbands and wives must #footnote[3\. Gain a good report.] First, take notice of the good qualities which are in one another, and, as one has occasion to speak of the other, make those good qualities the subject of their speech: as we #footnote[Prov 31:28, 29.] heard it commended before in the husband of that wife whom Solomon describes.

Secondly, they must lend a willing and joyful ear to those who (so far as they can tell) truly and sincerely, without flattery or hypocrisy, speak anything in commendation of the one or the other: not thinking themselves dispraised (which is the notion of many) when their bedfellow is praised, but rather having their hearts all the more enlarged to praise God for bestowing on them such an excellent token and pledge of his favor.

Thirdly, they must imitate those good things which they see, or hear to be, in one another, and imitate them in such a way that those who knew both husband and wife beforehand may say, “She learned this from him,” or, “He learned this from her.”

=== §. 35. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ wisdom in preserving each other’s good name.]

To #emph[preserve a good name,] it will be fitting for a husband or #footnote[4\. Preserve a good name.] wife wisely and at the right time to give one another notice of the good report that is raised of them, in order to provoke them both to give glory to God for it#footnote[Col 1:3, 4.] (as the apostle thanks God for the report which was spread abroad of the faith and love of the Colossians), and also to walk worthy of that good report (as the apostle, who had given great testimony of the generosity of the Corinthians, earnestly urges them to complete their generous gift: “Otherwise,” he says, “we … would be ashamed #footnote[2 Cor 9:4.] of this confident boasting”). For if those of whom a good report has once been raised decline, grow cold, fall back, or fall into notorious and scandalous sins, they will utterly extinguish and put out their good name among men, and turn it into an evil report, according to what Solomon says, “As dead flies bring a #footnote[Eccl 10:1.] stench to the perfumer’s oil, so does a little folly to him who is esteemed for wisdom and honor.” #footnote[5\. Show a care for one another’s good name.]

=== §. 36. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ fellow feeling for one another’s reputation.]

In the last place, to show a mutual provident care for one another’s good name, husbands and wives must be so moved by the report that goes about of either of them as if the report were of themselves. If the report is good, they must be glad of it and rejoice at it; if it is evil, they must be grieved, and troubled at it in a holy manner. In this way they will show a true sympathy and fellow feeling for one another’s reputation, according to that general rule of the apostle, “Rejoice with those who rejoice; weep with those who weep.”#footnote[Rom 12:15.]

=== §. 37. #emph[Of the vices contrary to that mutual care which man and wife should have for one another’s reputation.]

The vices contrary to these duties concerning the good name of #footnote[Contrary vices.] a husband and wife are, in general, two.

One is a readiness to disgrace and discredit one another, #footnote[1\. To discredit each other, and that] like Michal the wife of David,#footnote[2 Sam 6:20.] of whom we heard before. This is a hateful and detestable vice, which cannot stand with true marital love, but rather shows an utter dislike and a plain hatred of one another.

Husbands and wives discredit one another either by bringing on a bad name or by hindering a good name.

A bad name is brought on by the following means.

1\. By spreading abroad one another’s infirmities: as when #footnote[1\. By spreading one another’s infirmities abroad.] tattling gossips meet, their usual chatter is about their husbands, complaining of some vice or other in them. “My husband,” says one, “is covetous: I can hardly get anything from him: he makes me go dressed as nobody else does.” “And my husband,” replies another, “is so furious that no one knows how to speak to him.” So one after another goes on in this track, some revealing infirmities which should be concealed, others (which is worse) plainly slandering their husbands. In the same way husbands, when they meet with their boon companions, make their wives the common subject of all their talk, one accusing his wife of one vice, another his of another. There are two reasons why this vice (most detestable in itself) is made more hateful in a husband or a wife. 1. Because they know more of one another’s infirmities than anyone else does, so that if they are so evilly minded, they may discredit one another much more than anyone else can. 2. Because by their close union they are most bound to conceal and cover each other’s imperfections. Ham#footnote[Gen 9:22, 25.] was cursed for revealing his father’s nakedness. But husbands and wives who do this deserve a more horrible curse.

2\. By opening their ears to, and giving credence to, every slight #footnote[2\. By hearing and believing others’ bad reports.] report that anyone raises. More secret resentment of one against the other, and more open quarrels and contentions between them, commonly arise from this than from anything else.

3\. By twisting and misinterpreting one another’s actions, #footnote[3\. By turning everything to the worst.] words, yes, and thoughts also: taking everything in the worst sense.

4\. By concealing from one another the common evil rumors #footnote[4\. By concealing evil rumors.] which are raised about them, and are in every man’s mouth. Of all others, bedfellows are most fit to disclose such things to one another, and most bound to do it: most fit, because of their mutual familiarity; most bound, because of their close union.

Many husbands and wives hinder one another’s good #footnote[5\. By envying and contradicting good reports.] name by envying the good report that is made of the other, and contradicting it, as if the credit of the one must necessarily turn to the discredit of the other. Thus, as water quenches hot iron, so this envious disposition is a means to extinguish the heat of fame, and to put out the glorious light of a good name. And as by this they impair the reputation and honor of one another, so they monstrously discredit and dishonor themselves.

The other general vice of this kind is a careless disregard, #footnote[2\. Neglect of each other’s reputation.] or plain neglect, of one another’s reputation: when the husband is in no way moved by any report that goes about of his wife, nor the wife by any of the husband, but, as if they were mere strangers to one another, they pass by all reports made of one another. What mutual love can there be in such people? However their hands have been joined together, surely their hearts were never united; so that it would have been better if they had never known one another, unless the Lord afterwards knits their hearts and unites their affections more closely and firmly together.

=== §. 38. #emph[Of husbands’ and wives’ mutual provident care of the goods of the family.]

There remains yet one thing more about which husbands #footnote[Treatise 3, §. 29.] and wives ought to show a mutual provident care for each other, and that is the goods of this world. Although the husband, while he lives with his wife, has the truest ownership of them and the greatest title to them, yet I refer this to those mutual duties which man and wife owe to each other, in three respects.

First, because in conscience they belong to the wife’s use as well as to the husband’s.

Secondly, because the wife is appointed by God’s providence a joint governor of the family with the husband, and in that respect ought to be a help in providing such a sufficiency of the goods of this world as is needful for the condition in which God has set them, and for the charge which God has committed to them.

Thirdly, because the wife, if she outlives the husband, ought to have such a portion of those goods as is fitting for her place and charge.

In these respects we see that it is needful, indeed a binding duty, that husband and wife, even in mutual regard for one another, be as provident as they can be with a good conscience in getting, keeping and disposing of sufficient goods and riches for one another’s mutual good. Concerning the husband’s duty in this respect, no question is raised. The practice of all good husbands mentioned in Scripture, the care of providing for their #emph[own]#footnote[1 Tim 5:8. See Treatise 4, §. 46.] enjoined on them, their place and office as their wives’ #emph[head,] with many other such arguments (of which we will speak more distinctly when we come to set out the particular duties of husbands), prove as much. The greatest question concerns the wife, whether she is bound to take any care about the goods. But if the Scripture is thoroughly searched, we will find proof enough to show that she too is bound to it. For, first, the general end at which God aimed in making the woman (namely, to be “a helper”#footnote[Gen 2:18.] to man) implies as much: for in this she may be a very great help, as we will soon see in various particulars. 2. That general property attributed to a wife, to be “a good thing,”#footnote[Prov 18:22.] confirms as much: for what is profitable is called good; and it is one respect in which a wife is called a #emph[good thing,] that by her provident care and diligence she may bring much profit to her husband. Therefore in this respect, among others, the good wife whom Solomon describes is said #footnote[Prov 31:12.] to bring her husband “good” all the days of her life: for by her industry and provident care she so preserved and increased his goods that “the heart of her husband trusts in her, and he lacks nothing of value.”#footnote[#emph[v.] 11.] If the particular actions by which that good wife is described are well noted, we may easily observe that she was a special help to her husband, even in his outward condition. From all this we may infer these two points. First, that this provident care about outward, temporal goods is lawful, not unbecoming a Christian man or woman. Secondly, that it is a mutual duty belonging to both husband and wife.

For the first: how needful the goods of this world are for the preservation of life and health, the standing of the family, the good of church and commonwealth, the relief of the poor, and similar uses, no man can be ignorant. God has given them as blessings to his children, and that often in great abundance; and his children have accordingly been thankful for them. So a provident care about them is not unlawful, but very advisable and needful.

For the second: if there were not a joint care in this, the care and pains of the one might be altogether in vain. For suppose a husband is industrious, as Jacob#footnote[Gen 31:40.] was, and gets much abroad: if the wife, either by her lack of thrift, idleness, negligence or similar vices, allows what is brought home to be embezzled and wasted, or by her prodigality, love of finery or love of vain company consumes it herself, where will the profit of the husband’s pains be? Or on the other side, if a wife should be as hardworking and prosperous in getting as #footnote[Prov 31:10. #emph[etc.]] the good housewife mentioned before was, and the husband should waste it all away by card-playing, dicing, drinking, reveling or other such means, what fruit would remain of the wife’s provident care? In this mutual provident care of husband and wife, each of them must keep an eye on their own place. Affairs abroad belong most to the man, and are especially #footnote[Externa negotia viro permitte. Greg. Naz. ad Olymp.] to be ordered by him; what the wife is especially to care for is the business of the house. For the apostle lays it down as a rule for wives (as we will declare more particularly hereafter) that they be “keepers at home”#footnote[Titus 2:5.] and “govern their households.”#footnote[1 Tim 5:14.] By this means they may be very profitable to each other.

=== §. 39. #emph[Of the vices contrary to the good provident care of husband and wife for the goods of the family.]

Contrary to that duty are the following vices.

1\. #emph[Covetousness,] and excessive care for themselves: as #footnote[Contrary vices.] when a husband so rakes and scrapes and hoards up for himself that he neither gives his wife as much as is #footnote[1\. Covetousness.] fitting for her place while he lives with her, nor thinks of providing sufficient support for her if she outlives him, but rather thinks how to cheat her of what the law settles on her. Or when a wife secretly hoards up whatever she can get, either by her own industry or else by stealing from her husband: sometimes selling grain, wares, household goods or other such commodities so secretly that the husband will never know it; sometimes taking money out of his till, box, bag, chest or the like, so that either it will not be missed, or, if it is, it will not be known who had it. There are many who, distrusting their husbands’ provident care, or disliking them, or for some other ulterior motives, commit whatever they can get to the trust and keeping of others. By this it often comes about that they themselves, meeting with deceitful friends, are utterly cheated, simply because they dare not make their own fraud known. As covetousness is a hateful sin in itself, so it is made much more heinous by defrauding husband or wife, who ought to be as dear to each other as themselves.

2\. #emph[Prodigality,] and too lavish spending on themselves, #footnote[2\. Prodigality.] and on the things most agreeable to their own corrupt appetite: as when husbands, without any moderation or measure, spend their goods abroad in hunting, hawking, card-playing, dicing, eating, drinking or the like, and allow their wives to go in need at home; and yet they took wives whom they undertook to support, and for that reason received their portion. Or when wives bring their husbands into debt, and weaken, if not utterly overthrow, their estate by gorgeously decking and adorning their houses, by fine and costly clothing, by dainty fare, by gadding about to gossip, and the like. Many wives are so violent in this that if their desire and whim are not satisfied, their husbands will have no rest (of course they brought a portion, and maintained they must and will be; it does not matter whether their husbands’ estate can bear it or not), so much so that many are forced knowingly, for the sake of peace, to let their estate sink. O foolish and wretched wives! How little they consider that they were married to do their husbands good, and not harm, all the days of their life! Is this to be a help to man? Or rather, is it not utterly to thwart God’s counsel and pervert his purpose? Can we think that God will hold back, and not be avenged on them? Yet God will be avenged much more on the husbands named above, because of that image of himself which he has placed in them, and because of the place and authority in which he has set them. The apostle expressly says of them that each “is worse than an unbeliever”;#footnote[1 Tim 5:8.] and since that is so, they must look for the greater judgment.

3\. #emph[Idleness,] and a careless neglect of their estate. Many #footnote[3\. Idleness.] men spend day after day like a bird that flies up and down, as it happens, from tree to tree, from twig to twig. They go from place to place, but do not know for what end. As they meet with any company, so they stay as long as the company stays, and then look for other company, and are ready to go with anyone to the alehouse, tavern, playhouse, bowling alley or other such places. Many women also spend the whole morning lying in bed and dressing themselves, and the afternoon, as occasion offers, sitting idly at home or walking out to little purpose, only to wear away the time, paying little regard to their husbands’ estate, whether it increases or diminishes. Thus by the idleness and carelessness of husband and wife fair estates many times come to ruin, and both of them are brought to poverty and beggary.
